<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord Byron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as George Gordon Byron</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord Byron was an English Romantic poet, author of Childe Harold's Pilgrimage and Don Juan, and one of the most famous literary figures of his age. On his travels in 1809 he visited Ali Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepelene">Tepelenë</a>, an encounter he described in Childe Harold. He later supported the Greek War of Independence with money and leadership and died of fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> in 1824, becoming a hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-byron.jpg" alt="Lord Byron" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Phillips · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byron_1813_by_Phillips.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Lord Byron</b> was one of the few prominent defenders of the Luddites.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The cold, wet summer of 1816, which followed the eruption of Mount Tambora in Indonesia, kept <b>Lord Byron</b> and his guests indoors at the Villa Diodati by Lake Geneva, where his ghost-story contest led Mary Shelley to write Frankenstein.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lord Byron</b> paraphrased it in his Hebrew Melodies in 1815, and it inspired the chorus of Hebrew slaves, &quot;Va, pensiero&quot;, in Verdi's opera Nabucco (1842).</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a></li><li><blockquote>Isaac Nathan used the lively Sukkot tune in 1815 for <b>Lord Byron</b>'s &quot;The Wild Gazelle&quot;, one of the Hebrew Melodies.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li><li><blockquote>He negotiated with Napoleon and Britain, entertained <b>Lord Byron</b> in 1809, crushed the Souliotes and acquired Parga in 1819.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-yanina">Pashalik of Yanina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in City of Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.14&amp;year=1811" aria-label="City of Westminster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lord Byron"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.14&amp;year=1811">What was here in 1811? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is a pleasure in the pathless woods, There is a rapture on the lonely shore, There is society where none intrudes, By the deep Sea, and music in its roar: I love not Man the less, but Nature more…&#8221;</p><footer>Childe Harold's Pilgrimage, canto 4 (1818) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5131/pg5131-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The mountains look on Marathon— And Marathon looks on the sea; And musing there an hour alone, I dream’d that Greece might still be free; For standing on the Persians’ grave, I could not deem myself a slave.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Juan, canto 3 (1821), The Isles of Greece &#183; original &#183; the song of a poet in the poem &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/21700/pg21700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…but true; for truth is always strange; Stranger than fiction; if it could be told, How much would novels gain by the exchange!&#8221;</p><footer>Don Juan, canto 14 (1823) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/21700/pg21700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There was a sound of revelry by night, And Belgium's capital had gathered then Her Beauty and her Chivalry, and bright The lamps shone o'er fair women and brave men…&#8221;</p><footer>Childe Harold's Pilgrimage, canto 3 (1816), the eve of Waterloo &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5131/pg5131-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have not loved the world, nor the world me; I have not flattered its rank breath, nor bowed To its idolatries a patient knee…&#8221;</p><footer>Childe Harold's Pilgrimage, canto 3 (1816) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5131/pg5131-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
